# multi_assign_reject.tkr — the §9 F REJECT host. Four illegal forms live one per namespace under
# src/, and the single failing build reports every diagnostic it finds, one per scenario below. The
# sealed model has no first-class tuple: a multiple-return value is only ever destructured or returned,
# a parallel assignment's target/value counts must match, and an array decomposition admits at most one
# `..`. It has no successful build to sanitize/run.

Feature: §9 F reject — the compiler rejects the illegal multiple-binding / multiple-return forms

  Scenario: r1_arity — a parallel assignment whose target and value counts differ (ruling 1)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "the number of targets and values must match"

  Scenario: r2_value_position — a multiple-return value bound to a single name (ruling 3)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "a multiple-return value cannot be bound to a single name"

  Scenario: r4_double_rest — a second `..` skip-range in an array decomposition (ruling 2)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "at most one `..` skip-range in an array decomposition"

  Scenario: r5_single_return — an arity-1 `( A )` multiple-return signature (ruling 3)
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "needs at least two types"
